//! Ordinary differential equations on core's `Array`: adaptive initial
//! value solvers (explicit Runge-Kutta, Radau IIA, BDF, automatic stiff
//! switching) with dense output and events, fixed-step and symplectic
//! integrators, and boundary value problems by collocation.
//!
//! # Tutorial
//!
//! Many models say how a quantity changes rather than what it is: a
//! population grows in proportion to its size, a pendulum accelerates
//! back towards the bottom. These are ordinary differential equations,
//! `y' = f(t, y)`, and `ode` solves them. You write `f` as a closure;
//! the solver picks its step sizes to meet a tolerance and returns the
//! solution at its steps and as a function you can call at any time.
//!
//! ## A first equation
//!
//! Exponential decay, `y' = -0.5 y` with `y(0) = 10`, has the exact
//! solution `10 e^(-t/2)`. `solve_ivp` takes the right-hand side, the
//! time span as a range and the initial state. The state is a
//! `Vector<f64>`, even with one component.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use core::array::Vector;
//!
//! let f = |t: f64, y: Vector<f64>| -> Vector<f64> { y .* -0.5 };
//! let r = try ode::solve_ivp(f, 0.0..4.0, [10.0]);
//! println(f"{r.status:?} after {r.steps} steps, {r.nfev} evaluations");
//! println(f"y(4) = {r.last()[0]:.6}, exact {10.0 * (-2.0f64).exp():.6}");
//! # assert(r.status == Status::Finished);
//! # assert(r.steps == 4 && r.nfev == 26);
//! # assert((r.last()[0] - 1.354160).abs() < 5e-7);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! Finished after 4 steps, 26 evaluations
//! y(4) = 1.354160, exact 1.353353
//! ```
//!
//! The default method is `Rk45` (Dormand-Prince) with `rtol: 1e-3`,
//! enough for a plot. Ask for more with `rtol:` and `atol:`: the solver
//! keeps the local error below `atol + rtol * |y|`.
//!
//! ## Systems and the solution as a function
//!
//! A pendulum: angle `θ` and angular velocity `ω`, with `θ' = ω` and
//! `ω' = -(g / L) sin θ`. A second-order equation becomes a system of
//! two first-order ones. `r.sol` is the dense output, a function of
//! time, so the solution can be read anywhere on the span without
//! re-solving.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use core::array::{Vector, linspace};
//!
//! let g_over_l = 9.81 / 1.0;
//! let pendulum = |t: f64, y: Vector<f64>| -> Vector<f64> { [y[1], -g_over_l * y[0].sin()] };
//! let r = try ode::solve_ivp(pendulum, 0.0..10.0, [1.0, 0.0], rtol: 1e-9, atol: 1e-12);
//!
//! let sol = r.sol;
//! for t in [0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0] {
//!     println(f"t = {t}: angle {sol(t)[0]:>8.5} rad");
//! }
//! let ts: Vector<f64> = linspace(0.0, 10.0, 201);
//! let angles = sol(ts)[0, :];
//! println(f"{angles.len()} points for a plot, smallest {angles.min() ?? 0.0:.5}");
//! # assert((sol(1.0)[0] + 0.98007).abs() < 5e-6);
//! # assert((sol(2.0)[0] - 0.92079).abs() < 5e-6);
//! # assert(angles.len() == 201);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! t = 0.5: angle  0.10426 rad
//! t = 1: angle -0.98007 rad
//! t = 1.5: angle -0.30787 rad
//! t = 2: angle  0.92079 rad
//! 201 points for a plot, smallest -0.99998
//! ```
//!
//! `sol(t)` at one time gives the state as a vector; at a vector of
//! times, a matrix with one row per component, so `[0, :]` is the angle
//! at every time.
//!
//! ## Events
//!
//! An event is a function of the state whose zero the solver finds
//! exactly. Here a ball is thrown upwards, and the event `ground` stops
//! the run when it lands; `t_events` holds when each event happened.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use core::array::Vector;
//!
//! let fall = |t: f64, y: Vector<f64>| -> Vector<f64> { [y[1], -9.81] };
//! let ground = Event { f: |t: f64, y: Vector<f64>| -> f64 { y[0] }, direction: Direction::Falling, terminal: true };
//! let r = try ode::solve_ivp(fall, 0.0..10.0, [0.0, 15.0], events: [ground], rtol: 1e-10);
//! let landed = r.t_events[0][0];
//! println(f"lands after {landed:.4} s, exact {2.0 * 15.0 / 9.81:.4} s");
//! # assert(r.status == Status::Event);
//! # assert((landed - 2.0 * 15.0 / 9.81).abs() < 1e-8);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! lands after 3.0581 s, exact 3.0581 s
//! ```
//!
//! ## Stiff equations
//!
//! Some systems mix very fast and very slow change, such as chemical
//! reactions with rates many orders of magnitude apart. An explicit
//! method like `Rk45` must take tiny steps to stay stable there, even
//! where the solution barely moves. Implicit methods (`Radau`, `Bdf`,
//! `Rodas`) take large steps, and `Auto` switches between the two kinds
//! as the problem changes. Robertson's reaction is the classic test.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use core::array::Vector;
//!
//! let robertson = |t: f64, y: Vector<f64>| -> Vector<f64> {
//!     [-0.04 * y[0] + 1e4 * y[1] * y[2],
//!      0.04 * y[0] - 1e4 * y[1] * y[2] - 3e7 * y[1] * y[1],
//!      3e7 * y[1] * y[1]]
//! };
//! let y0: Vector<f64> = [1.0, 0.0, 0.0];
//! for m in [Method::Bdf, Method::Radau, Method::Auto] {
//!     let r = try ode::solve_ivp(robertson, 0.0..1e5, y0, method: m, rtol: 1e-6, atol: 1e-10);
//!     let y = r.last();
//!     println(f"{m:?}: {r.steps} steps, y = [{y[0]:.4}, {y[1]:.3e}, {y[2]:.4}]");
//! #   assert((y[0] - 0.0179).abs() < 5e-5 && (y[2] - 0.9821).abs() < 5e-5);
//! #   assert((y[1] - 7.275e-8).abs() < 5e-11);
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! Bdf: 327 steps, y = [0.0179, 7.275e-8, 0.9821]
//! Radau: 188 steps, y = [0.0179, 7.275e-8, 0.9821]
//! Auto: 302 steps, y = [0.0179, 7.275e-8, 0.9821]
//! ```
//!
//! ## Boundary value problems
//!
//! Not every problem starts from a known state: a beam is fixed at both
//! ends, a temperature is held at both sides of a wall. `solve_bvp`
//! takes the equation, a function of both ends that is zero when the
//! conditions hold, a mesh and a first guess. Here a cable hanging
//! between two posts, `y'' = sqrt(1 + y'^2) / a`, with `y = 0` at both
//! ends.
//!
//! ```nabi
//! use core::array::{Array, Matrix, Vector, linspace};
//!
//! let a = 2.0;
//! let cable = |x: f64, y: Vector<f64>| -> Vector<f64> { [y[1], (1.0 + y[1] * y[1]).sqrt() / a] };
//! let ends = |ya: Vector<f64>, yb: Vector<f64>| -> Vector<f64> { [ya[0], yb[0]] };
//! let x: Vector<f64> = linspace(-1.0, 1.0, 11);
//! let guess: Matrix<f64> = Array::zeros([2, 11]);
//! let b = try ode::solve_bvp(cable, ends, x, guess, tol: 1e-8);
//!
//! // The exact shape is a catenary: a cosh(x / a) - a cosh(1 / a).
//! let sag = b.sol.at(0.0)[0];
//! println(f"{b.success()}: sag at the middle {sag:.6}, exact {a - a * (1.0 / a).cosh():.6}");
//! # assert(b.success());
//! # assert((sag - (a - a * (1.0 / a).cosh())).abs() < 1e-6);
//! ```
//!
//! ```text
//! true: sag at the middle -0.255252, exact -0.255252
//! ```
//!
